  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/cherax-peknyi-2/",
      "html": "<p>Cherax peknyi Lukhaup &amp; Herbert, 2008, the zebra lobster, is a colourful freshwater crayfish from New Guinea. It is easy to keep and breed in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/cherax-peknyi-2/",
      "html": "<p>The basic food for all of them is dead leaves from trees, but they also accept practically all types of fish food accepted by ornamental fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/cherax-peknyi-2/",
      "html": "<p>They always try to hide and usually leave their hiding places only at night and when they are fed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/cherax-peknyi-2/",
      "html": "<p>These crayfish were the subject of several changes in determination, because the freshwater crayfish from New Guinea were hardly known before they appeared as aquarium inhabitants. Initially they were imported under the name C. misolicus, but it was guessed they might be closer to C. papuanus. The valid name is now Cherax peknyi Lukhaup &amp; Herbert, 2008.</p>"
    }
  ],
